<p class='text'>In 2014, Giancarlo Stanton  <a href='https://www.espn.co.uk/mlb/story/_/id/11902473/giancarlo-stanton-signs-record-325m-deal-miami-marlins'>signed a huge deal</a> with the Miami Marlins for 13 years starting in 2015.  Implying he would play for them until the end of 2027 (at the time of writing, that is still three more seasons).  After just three seasons, he was traded to the Yankees, with the Marlins still paying a portion of that salary.  His career may now be over, leaving both the Marlins and the Yankees on the hook for the rest of the money with no benefit.</p>
<p class='text'>How stupid is that?</p>
<p class='text'>Stupid, perhaps, but sadly not atypical of sports contracts.</p>
<p class='text'>For example, I believe there was a point at which Manchester United were firing coaches so regularly that they were paying for their current manager along with two who had been offered long-term contracts but then been fired.</p>
<p class='text'>The Phillies spent a lot of money re-sigining star slugger Ryan Howard, although for just three years, when it was already obvious that his best years were behind him.  They ended up benching him and then paying him to not even turn up for his final season.</p>
<p class='text'>Gareth Bale signed for big money at Real Madrid, only to spend much of his five years there watching from the stands - to nobody's benefit - because the coach did not want to play him and no other team was prepared to pick up his salary.</p>
<p class='text'>Marcus Rashford has recently been benched by United - and ultimately sent out on loan - because the team do not want to play him but cannot get anyone else to take him on and pay his salary.</p>
<p class='text'>The problem is in the incentives for the parties concerned.</p>
<p class='text'>Players (and their agents) are understandably interested in making the most amount of money that they can.  And a longer contract guarantees that the player will receive more money, since the payments are guaranteed to continue for as long as the contract does.</p>
<p class='text'>Teams want to sign the best players and, understandably, the best players are the most expensive and the most in demand.  They can, in general, hold out for the terms and conditions that they want.  Once the best players have been taken off the market, the next tranche of players are now "the best" and can hold out for what they want - maybe not the same amount, but still quite possibly ridiculous.</p>
<p class='text'>But none of this is connected to <i>reality</i>:  contracts are signed in advance, but reality only emerges after the fact.  Contracts are signed out of optimism; payments still have to be made well after disillusionment and regret have settled in.</p>
<p class='text'>It should not be this way.  But no one team can buck the trend - they are just frozen out by the best players.</p>
<p class='text'>Elsewhere I have written about European "Financial Fair Play" (FFP) rules and principles by which they might work; here I am going to assume that clubs have the money they think they have but don't have a crystal ball to see into the future.  Nothing I write here addresses the fundamental inequality in income between teams.  I touch on other aspects of that elsewhere.</p>
<p class='text'>It's really very simple: don't sign contracts with a time element.  A contract can be signed for any amount of money (per week or month) but all contracts are rolling contracts until terminated by either party or by the intervention of a third party.</p>
<p class='text'>If the team and player agree to mutually terminate a contract, it is voided with immediate effect, and the only payment is any outstanding salary for work that has already been performed but not yet paid.</p>
<p class='text'>In the event that a team wishes to terminate a player, the league should stipulate a fixed "compensation" period.  The player ceases to be on that team with immediate effect and can immediately look for another position and sign a contract.  A reasonable "compensation period" would be a minimum of three months or until the end of the season, whichever comes first, but I would leave it up to each league to decide their own rules.</p>
<p class='text'>If a player wishes to terminate their contract, they are welcome to do so at any time, but all payments to the player will cease immediately.  They must then wait the same amount of time as the "compensation period" would have been if they had been fired before they can sign another contract, although they are allowed to enter into negotations with any other teams in the meantime.</p>
<p class='text'>When a player signs a new contract, it is not affected in any practical way by the contract just voided (it may be for more or less money and have different performance criteria).</p>
<p class='text'>Finally, it is possible for a third party (another team) to approach both team and player and ask to buy out the contract of any given player.  Both team and player must consent before this goes forward.  In this case they must compensate the selling team with the same amount of "compensation money" along with any other amounts that may be needed (a transfer fee) to obtain the consent of the originating team.  If such consent is not forthcoming, a player may opt to terminate their contract and take a three month hiatus before signing the new contract.  While the new contract is not required to match the terms of the previous contract, most players will probably not sign a contract of lesser value.</p>
<p class='text'>Contracts should not just be for fixed sums, but should include performance clauses: there should be a basic salary, but also payments on the basis of appearances (or time spent playing), contributions to victories and such metrics as the team and player may agree on.  While it would be hard in the abstract to determine which metrics would be most appropriate for each sport and position, over time I would expect this to grow into a cottage industry.  These metrics would be solely used for decisions about payment, not about retention of players: these contracts are now "labour at will".</p>
<p class='text'>It is possible that players will encounter teams that act in bad faith: promising large appearance fees and then deliberately not selecting the player.  Leaving aside the reputational damage (especially if all contracts were required to be a matter of public record) and thus the problems they would have in the future hiring players, players would have the ultimate sanction to quit.  While losing three months' salary would not be desirable for most players, it would certainly be preferable to continuing with a toxic contract.  Allowing all contracts to be voided "at the end of the season" would also give players an opportunity once a year to escape such situations with minimal cost while also doing the least amount of damage to clubs if the players act in bad faith.</p>
